<!--
    loweredswitchexact (GH-468 finding 4 soundness): install a re-rolled lowered
    switch only when it is the compare tree it replaces.

    Two ways the install differed from the tree, both x86-64 gcc -O2 from the RE
    corpus, with the tiny callees stubbed to return their first argument:

    classify @ 0x2548b: a nested switch over minor() whose values 0x72 and 0x91
    share one body (both return 0xfffffffc). The install kept one case label per
    body, so value 0x91 lost its label and fell into the default. With the option
    on every case value keeps its label.

    pick_table @ 0x27e90: a CMOV-selected dispatch whose values 2 and 6 share one
    body. The install kept one label per body, so value 6 lost its label, and it
    deleted the compare block that zeroes R8D (`xor r8d,r8d ; cmp eax,6`) for the
    default's `mov eax,r8d ; ret`, so pass 1's default returns an uninitialized
    fifth argument (`return a4;`). With the option on the raw p-code check declines
    the install and the compare tree returns 0.

    g3 @ 0x401000: gcc 11 -O2 over an `unsigned char` switch with cases 129 and
    200, linked with stub callees. The selector is typed `char` after the install
    records unsigned labels, so pass 1 prints `switch(a0)` over `char a0` with
    `case 0x81:`, a value a signed char never has. Pass 2 casts the selector to the
    labels' signedness.

    f @ 0x401059: hand-written x86-64 reduced from gcc -Os, where a compare block
    loads a case body's argument (`mov esi,7 ; cmp edi,200 ; je T200` and
    `T200: mov edi,esi ; jmp hv`, `T300: lea edi,[rsi+1] ; jmp hw`). The simplified
    graph propagates the 7 into both bodies, so the load is no statement of the
    compare block there, but the install edits raw p-code and deletes it. Pass 1
    prints `hv(a1)` and `hw(a1 + 1)`; pass 2 declines the install.

    rlater @ 0x402000: the same shape behind an unrelated guard and call, so its
    cascade is found from a later head (`loweredswitchheads`). A later head is
    checked as this option checks every cascade even when the option is off, so
    both passes decline its install and print `hv(7)` and `hw(8)`.

    Pass 1 (option loweredswitchexact off): classify shows only case 0x72,
    pick_table returns a4, g3 switches on a signed char, f loses the loaded 7.
    Pass 2 (default, on): classify labels both 0x72 and 0x91, pick_table keeps its
    compare tree, g3 casts its selector, f passes 7 and 8.

    The routing checks that decline a switch whose install would mis-route a value
    are exercised by the module unit tests.
-->
